<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bohemond VI of Antioch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1237–<a href="/years/1275">1275</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Bohemond VI · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnulfids">Ramnulfids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bohemond VI was Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and Count of Tripoli from 1252. In 1260 he submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> and joined their advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, which brought papal excommunication and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> hostility. In 1268 the Mamluk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> stormed Antioch while Bohemond was in Tripoli, ending the principality after 170 years.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bohemond VI was the son of Bohemond V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and Lucienne of Segni, a great-niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a>. When his father died he was fifteen, and he succeeded under the regency of his mother. Lucienne never left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and handed the government of the principality to her relatives, which made her unpopular. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, then on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, helped the young prince to obtain permission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> to inherit a few months early.</p><p>Louis knighted him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, and he took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. At Louis's suggestion, in 1254 Bohemond married Sibylla, daughter of King Hetoum I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, which ended a power struggle between the two states that had begun under his grandfather Bohemond IV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As overlord of the Genoese Embriaco family, Bohemond was drawn into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-saint-sabas">War of Saint Sabas</a> between the Genoese and the Venetians, which began in 1256. The Embriaco lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Gibelet</a> rebelled against him in 1258, and the dispute became a civil war. Hetoum persuaded him to submit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and in 1260 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> became a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. Both rulers took part with their own forces in the Mongol conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> that year.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> rewarded Bohemond with towns that had been lost to the Muslims, among them Lattakieh and Jabala, on condition that he install a Greek patriarch, Euthymius, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> one. This earned him the enmity of the Latins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, and the Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Jacques Pantaléon</a>, excommunicated him. After Pantaléon became Pope Urban IV he suspended the sentence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> defeated the Mongols at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a> in September 1260, and their leader Baibars began to threaten Antioch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1266 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> army defeated the army of Cilician Armenia at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>. In 1268 Baibars besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and took it while Bohemond was away in Tripoli; all of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> was soon lost, leaving him with no estates except Tripoli. In 1271 Baibars besieged Tripoli and sent Bohemond a letter taunting him for his alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, but in May offered a truce, as the Mamluks had heard of a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> under the prince who would later be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>.</p><p>Bohemond died in 1275, leaving a son and three daughters. The son, Bohemond VII, was nominal prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, though Antioch had ceased to exist, and count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>. Lucia later became titular countess of Tripoli. Mamluk rancour at his alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> lasted until the fall of Tripoli in 1289.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bohemond VI of Antioch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 13th century it was united with the County of Tripoli, and <b>Bohemond VI</b> submitted to the Mongols in 1260.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Principality of Antioch</a></li></ul></section>
